---

Acknowledgements. No code from the works below is included in this Software; its
techniques were written from scratch after them, and they are credited here with thanks:

  The cloth:
    T. Jakobsen, "Advanced Character Physics", Game Developers Conference 2001.
    M. Mueller, B. Heidelberger, M. Hennix and J. Ratcliff, "Position Based Dynamics",
      Journal of Visual Communication and Image Representation 18 (2007).
    T.-Y. Kim, N. Chentanez and M. Mueller-Fischer, "Long Range Attachments -- A Method
      to Simulate Inextensible Clothing in Computer Games", SCA 2012.
    M. Macklin, K. Storey, M. Lu, P. Terdiman, N. Chentanez, S. Jeschke and M. Mueller,
      "Small Steps in Physics Simulation", SCA 2019.
    The world velocity and inertia scales of NVIDIA PhysX's and Unity's cloth -- the idea
      that a moved attachment drags its cloth only in part; none of their code.
  The cloth against itself:
    M. Macklin, M. Mueller, N. Chentanez and T.-Y. Kim, "Unified Particle Physics for
      Real-Time Applications", ACM Transactions on Graphics 33(4) (SIGGRAPH 2014).
    M. Mueller, "Ten Minute Physics" 15, "Self-collisions" (2022) -- a thickness as wide as
      the spacing.
    M. Teschner, B. Heidelberger, M. Mueller, D. Pomeranets and M. Gross, "Optimized
      Spatial Hashing for Collision Detection of Deformable Objects", VMV 2003.
    L. Verlet, "Computer 'Experiments' on Classical Fluids. I.", Physical Review 159
      (1967) -- the neighbour list.
    P. Volino and N. Magnenat-Thalmann, "Efficient Self-Collision Detection on Smoothly
      Discretised Surface Animations using Geometrical Shape Regularity", Computer
      Graphics Forum 13(3) (Eurographics 1994) -- the normal cone.
  The air on the cloth:
    M. J. Lighthill, "Note on the swimming of slender fish", J. Fluid Mech. 9 (1960).
    C. Eloy, C. Souilliez and L. Schouveiler, "Flutter of a rectangular plate",
      J. Fluids Struct. 23 (2007).
    M. Argentina and L. Mahadevan, "Fluid-flow-induced flutter of a flag", PNAS 102 (2005).
    M. J. Shelley and J. Zhang, "Flapping and bending bodies interacting with fluid
      flows", Annu. Rev. Fluid Mech. 43 (2011).
    S. Taneda, "Waving motions of flags", J. Phys. Soc. Japan 24 (1968).
    P. Causin, J.-F. Gerbeau and F. Nobile, "Added-mass effect in the design of
      partitioned algorithms for fluid-structure problems", CMAME 194 (2005).
    S. F. Hoerner, "Fluid-Dynamic Drag" (1965).
    L. Prandtl's turbulent flat-plate friction law, in H. Schlichting, "Boundary-Layer
      Theory" -- the friction along the cloth.
  The wind:
    EN 1991-1-4, Eurocode 1: Actions on structures -- Wind actions.
    H. A. Panofsky and J. A. Dutton, "Atmospheric Turbulence" (1984).
    G. I. Taylor, "The spectrum of turbulence" (1938); A. N. Kolmogorov (1941).
  The fabric:
    A. C. Estevez and C. Kulla, "Production Friendly Microfacet Sheen BRDF" (2017).
    D. Neubelt and M. Pettineo, "Crafting a Next-Gen Material Pipeline for The Order:
      1886", SIGGRAPH 2013 course notes.
    S. Green, "Real-Time Approximations to Subsurface Scattering", GPU Gems ch. 16 (2004).
    S. Lagarde, "Water drop 3b - Physically based wet surfaces" (2013, blog) -- the cloth
      darker when wet.
    D. Lazarov, "Physically-based lighting in Call of Duty: Black Ops", SIGGRAPH 2011
      course notes -- the gloss-to-exponent mapping of the water's film.
  The drawn surface:
    E. Catmull and R. Rom, "A class of local interpolating splines", Computer Aided
      Geometric Design (1974).
    G. Taubin, "A Signal Processing Approach to Fair Surface Design", SIGGRAPH 1995 --
      the edges' smoothing.
  The gizmo:
    the idea of Procedural Objects' 3-axis gizmos (a Cities: Skylines mod) and of 3-D
      editors' transform handles -- the idea only; none of their code.
  Picking a flag:
    C. Ericson, "Real-Time Collision Detection" (2005) -- the closest points of two
      segments and the ray against a turned box: the flags' pick volumes.
    M. Woo, J. Neider, T. Davis and D. Shreiner, "OpenGL Programming Guide", ch.
      "Selection and Feedback" -- the picking region a few pixels round the cursor
      (gluPickMatrix): the least a flag's pick target is on the screen.
    U. Zoelzer, "Digital Audio Signal Processing", 2nd ed. (2008), "Dynamic Range
      Control" -- the peak measurement's instant attack and exponential release: the
      pick target held over the flag's flutter.
  A flag hung upright:
    The United States Flag Code, 4 U.S.C. 7(i) -- "the union should be uppermost and to
      the flag's own right": the design of a flag hoisted from its top.
  Still flags:
    The Met Office, the Beaufort wind force scale -- force 3, "light flags extended": the
      breeze a still flag is caught in.
    W. Froude's similarity law (the Froude number, U^2 / gL) -- the breeze for a bigger flag.
    W. Welch and A. Witkin, "Variational Surface Modeling", SIGGRAPH 1992 -- the thin-plate
      energy (squared second derivatives): the smoothest moment of a still flag kept.
  The player's pictures:
    the images folders of Procedural Objects and Image Overlay (Cities: Skylines mods) -- a
      folder button and a list of the files: the idea only; none of their code.
    S. J. Gortler, R. Grzeszczuk, R. Szeliski and M. F. Cohen, "The Lumigraph", SIGGRAPH
      1996 -- the pull-push: a picture's see-through pixels given its visible colours.
    The PNG specification (W3C Recommendation / ISO/IEC 15948) and ITU-T T.81 (JPEG) -- a
      picture's size read from its header.
  And:
    K. Perlin, "Improving Noise", SIGGRAPH 2002 -- the quintic fade.
    C. Wellons, the lowbias32 integer hash.
    G. Fiedler, "Fix Your Timestep!" (2004).
    J. Hable, the "Uncharted 2" filmic curve (2010) -- the preview's tone mapping only.
